    "text": "This question is more general, so I'll just leave a possible hint. Generally it's your responsibility while coding your solution to check such things. Good that you see that such problems could be possible. You have many materials available: Linux device drivers book (also linked in course materials), while outdated, has a whole chapter on memory mapping. Then you have kernel documentation. If it's your way of doing things there's Perplexity/ChatGPT with search, but here be careful because it can say untrue things - happened to me. Finally you can also check some existing driver's code in the kernel - some are simple enough.",
